            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, which the main source is coming from the crawlspace (up through the floor). 

Many of the windows do not appear to close correctly, leaving rather large gaps for cold air to infiltrate into the home. Several of the hinge/operating hardware seems damaged or broken. Weather stripping is also either missing or in rough shape. One of the upgrades I've modeled further on in the report is suggesting new windows. If you decide to keep these current ones, ensure the windows can close properly, and with good contact on the weather stripping. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along both of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal. The back door does not contact/close very tightly making it difficult to contact any new weather stripping installed. An adjustable strike plate could be used to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 

As mentioned above, the crawlspace is the largest source of air leakage within the home (and I suspect this likely leads to the floors feeling quite cold). Based on the design of the crawlspace, I suspect this area is not meant to be directly heated. The floor has been completely insulated, however not air sealed, which allows cold/warm air to move freely through the floor joists. Fiberglass batt is more effective when air is not able to pass directly through it (fiberglass batt has poor air sealing capabilities). I recommend to repair the air seal along the underside of the floor. One thing to note with this recommendation is the location of the hot water heater. Please see the hot water heater upgrade section for further comments on this. 



Savings: For a minimum 20% improvement you could potentially save $230 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.1 GJ, and qualify for up to$750 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.

I suspect after tackling the crawlspace air sealing, this will likely improve significantly. 



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R20 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.

The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R20 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $690 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $8,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house are older single pane wood windows (with a storm pane installed on the outside), that are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows (8 in total) with ones that meet the BTU per hour criteria listed above, you could potentially save $740 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 250 per window in rebates (12 windows maximum) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. Although the home likely does not currently experience signs of low ventilation, as the high air leakage encourages a natural ventilation to occur. However, as the home becomes tighter with these renovations, this will become evident very quickly. 



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



An HRV saves on energy costs compared to conventional ventilation systems because it recovers heat from exhausted air.  The HRV draws in stale, indoor air and passes it through a heat exchanger. The exchanger captures most of the heat before it exhausts the stale air outside. 



At the same time, the HRV draws outdoor air in for filtering, passes it through the other half of the heat exchanger where it collects heat from the exhausted air, and finally distributes the outdoor air throughout the house. The warmed, outdoor air is distributed through an existing forced-air distribution system, or a dedicated ductwork system. 



When purchasing an HRV, choose a model that is certified by the Heating and Ventilating Institute (HVI) and consider models that have a high efficiency motor to help reduce electrical consumption. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 189 litre (estimated) electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1,100 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



HOT WATER HEATER LOCATION

Currently, the hot water heater is located in the crawlspace. While the escaping heat from the floor above is currently keeping the crawlspace at a reasonable temperature, a hot water heater should be located within the heated space of the home. Whether you decide to stick with an electric hot water tank, or replace it with an on-demand high efficiency model, I recommend relocating the hot water heater. As it's current location is a high risk for freeze ups (damaging the tank), as well as the colder temperature forces the heater to operate more often to maintain the set temperature. </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $1,350 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the furnace will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a furnace rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

The following are the estimated rebates per component of the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation (R20 additional wall insulation)

     - $2,000 for the windows (8 windows)

     - $3,000 for the heating system, Furnace (95% high efficiency with ECM fan motor)

     - $750 for the air leakage reduction (estimated 20% reduction)

     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate (43% reduction in GJ rating, based on all upgrades listed above)



In addition to the rebates from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:



     - ESTAR fridge &amp; washing machine - $200 each

     - ESTAR ventless (condensing) clothes dryer - $400

     - ECM Pumps (the water circulation pump) - $175

     - Programmable Thermostats - 25% up to $175 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - LED Lighting - see next section.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



